" Command reference: https://jb.gg/h38q75

let mapleader = " "
let maplocalleader = ","
" Mirrors vim.g.maplocalleader = "," in ~/.config/nvim/init.lua, so localleader
" keys (git, typst, ...) type as `,*` on both sides.
" DEAD KEY: IdeaVim never expands <localleader>. vim-engine's
" VimStringParserBase resolves one special name, "leader", by reading
" g:mapleader. There is no localleader branch, so any mapping written with
" <localleader> does nothing at all. Every localleader key in this file
" therefore hardcodes a literal `,`, e.g. `nmap ,gd`. The `let` above only
" records intent and costs nothing if IdeaVim ever adds support.
set incsearch
set notimeout
set timeoutlen=5000

" === Plugins ===
" Two groups, split by whether the plugin needs a Marketplace install. Names
" follow the official emulated-plugins doc:
" https://github.com/JetBrains/ideavim/blob/master/doc/IdeaVim%20Plugins.md

" --- Built-in (IdeaVim emulates these; `set` is enough, nothing to install) ---
set surround
" The `let` must precede the `set`: the extension reads it in init(), the same
" constraint anyobject and quickscope have.
" 2.45.0 aligned the default mappings with the original plugin (VIM-2178, a
" breaking change). <C-n> gets swallowed in N/V/O and steals the IDE keymap's
" C-n. Worse, skip-occurrence lands on <C-x> by default, which would eat this
" file's entire chord prefix. Our <A-n>/<A-x>/<A-p> are bound explicitly below,
" so the defaults contribute nothing but damage. <Plug>* registration is
" independent of this switch and keeps working.
let g:multi_cursor_use_default_mapping = 0
set multiple-cursors

set commentary
set exchange
set highlightedyank
set matchit
set vim-paragraph-motion          " { / } skip whitespace-only lines

" --- Structural text objects and navigation: prefer IdeaVim's built-ins ---
" The built-ins run on IntelliJ PSI (functextobj, classtextobj, [m ]m) or on
" plain-text algorithms (mini-ai, argtextobj, indentwise, CamelCaseMotion).
" None of them guesses what language a file is written in. That guess is what
" breaks AnyObject; see "AnyObject shrink" below.
"
" ORDERING FACT: an extension's init() does not run at its `set` line. IdeaVim
" runs every init() after the whole rc finishes, in `set` order. Two
" consequences:
"   1. A `map` in this file cannot stop a later-initialising extension from
"      force-overriding it through putKeyMapping. Writing order protects
"      nothing.
"   2. Changing a g:* that an already-enabled extension read in init() has no
"      effect on :source, because the option never flipped so init() never
"      reran. Use `:set no<name>` then `:source`, or restart the IDE.
" Rule: enable only extensions with IfMissing semantics. A key belongs to an
" extension because nothing else claims it, never because of load order.
"
" FORBIDDEN: targets.vim. It registers n/l seek variants for every object,
" claiming the whole an*/in*/al*/il* prefix space. That collides with
" al/il=@loop and an/in=indent block. The complete mapping doubles as a targets
" prefix, so under `set notimeout` a `dal` hangs forever waiting for a second
" key. Taking targets would mean moving loop off l, which gives up parity.
" Accepted cost: no I/A modifiers, no n/l seek. anyitem covers separator
" objects. Unblock condition: IdeaVim ports g:targets_nl so n/l can move
" elsewhere. Still unsupported at 2.46, and targets has shipped without
" settings since it went built-in in 2.39. Recheck on upgrade.
"
" FORBIDDEN: textobj-indent. Its init() calls putKeyMapping, not IfMissing, and
" binds ai/aI/ii unconditionally with no switch. By the ordering fact above it
" always shadows conditional, and ownership drifts with init order. Indent
" blocks go to anyobject's anyindentblock instead: a pure line-indent algorithm
" that never consults language detection, and just as reliable. Accepted cost:
" no aI variant, and an excludes the header line above the block. Unblock
" condition: upstream switches to IfMissing or adds a default-key switch.
" Neither exists at 2.46. A second route opened up in 2.45, which shipped
" built-in textobj-user: it declares an indent object on keys you choose,
" with no forced mapping. Try that first if you want to migrate back.
set mini-ai                       " aq/iq any quote, ab/ib any bracket
set argtextobj                    " aa/ia argument (<Plug>{Inner,Outer}Argument)
set functextobj                   " am/aM/im function objects, remapped to af/if below
set classtextobj                  " ac class outer; inner comes from anyobject's iC
set textobj-entire                " ae/ie whole file
set indentwise                    " [- ]- [+ ]+ [= ]= [% ]% navigate by indent level
set CamelCaseMotion               " g:camelcasemotion_key deliberately unset, so it
                                  " generates no <leader>w/b/e/ge defaults. Only its
                                  " inner-subword <Plug> gets bound, to iu below.

" --- Marketplace (install the plugin in Settings > Plugins first, or the `set` fails silently) ---
set easymotion                    " AceJump + IdeaVim-EasyMotion
set which-key                     " Which-Key
" === AnyObject shrink (28333) ===
" Keep only the seven objects the built-ins have no equivalent for. Everything
" else moved to the built-in extensions above. The key-ownership table at the
" end of the "Syntax-aware" section lists who owns what.
"
" Why shrink it (read from v3.1.4's TSLanguageUtils source): AnyObject guesses
" the language itself. It checks a file-suffix whitelist first and never asks
" the IDE for the file type, then compares PSI displayName exactly without
" walking baseLanguage, which drops every dialect such as .pyi, .bzl and .pyx.
" It finally falls back to the IDE name, and that fallback always returns a
" hit. Because the fallback can never miss, guessing wrong does not leave the
" buffer alone. It parses the file with the wrong grammar, produces an ERROR
" tree, and every selection and ]/[ jump lands somewhere arbitrary. That is
" v3.1.4 behaviour. The plugin iterates fast, so if language detection gets
" fixed (PSI first, baseLanguage fallback), this shrink can be relaxed again.
"
" Kept: l=loop  i=conditional  k=comment  v=variable
"       y=list item (targets is forbidden, nothing else offers a separator object)
"       n=indent block (textobj-indent is forbidden; this one is plain-text, the
"         only object that a wrong language guess cannot affect)
"       C=class, for inner only. Outer uses native ac, which is PSI-backed and
"         reliable. iC is currently the only source of class-inner. Lowercase
"         ic stays unbound; when classtextobj grows an inner object, ic goes
"         to it.
" Three semantics given up on purpose: anystring (mini-ai's quote objects
" approximate it), a dedicated lambda key (functextobj's heuristic swallows
" it), and the merged arg|item key (aa and ay cover the two halves).
"
" The `let`s must precede the `set`. After changing one, re-init per point 2 of
" the ordering fact or it will not take effect. i and y are swapped to match
" nvim (textobjects.lua binds ai/ii=@conditional). anyindentblock already
" defaults to n, so it needs no variable.
let g:anyobject_included = "anyloop,anyconditional,anycomment,anyvariable,anyitem,anyclass,anyindentblock"
let g:anyobject_map_anyconditional = "i"
let g:anyobject_map_anyitem = "y"
let g:anyobject_map_anyclass = "C"
set anyobject
" anyclass brings ]C/[C (jump between classes in the buffer) back with it,
" which happens to match nvim's ]C/[C=@class.outer. They fail in files whose
" language AnyObject guesses wrong; use Cmd-Up or the Structure panel there.
set peekaboo                      " Vim Peekaboo (25776). " / @ / <C-r> preview registers
" Lazy mode: no highlight until you press f/F/t/T, which then marks the best
" jump target on the current line. Mirrors highlight_on_key = true in nvim's
" eyeliner.nvim (lua/plugins/edit/eyeliner.lua).
" The `let` must precede `set quickscope`, same constraint as anyobject.
let g:qs_highlight_on_keys = ['f', 'F', 't', 'T']
set quickscope                    " IdeaVim-Quickscope (19417). Highlights unique f/F/t/T targets
set keep-english-in-normal        " IdeaVimExtension (9615). Switches to a Latin IME in Normal mode

" f/t/F/T reverse-repeat moves to `\`, mirroring lua/core/keymaps.lua.
" maplocalleader took the native `,` and turned it into the prefix for a whole
" family of keys: `,g*`, `,d*`, `,m*`. This file also sets `notimeout`, so a
" bare `,` never falls back on its own. You would have to type a second key
" that forms no mapping before the native reverse-repeat runs, and by then the
" cursor has already moved. So `,` becomes a plain <Nop> prefix, the same
" treatment <C-x> gets, and reverse-repeat goes to `\`, which has no native
" normal command and is unclaimed on both sides.
" `\` uses noremap, so the `,` on its right-hand side keeps native semantics
" and ignores the <Nop> below. `;` keeps native forward-repeat.
" These use the three explicit modes rather than `noremap`/`map`, which would
" also capture Select mode. In a selection, `\` and `,` should replace the
" selected text as usual.
nnoremap \ ,
xnoremap \ ,
onoremap \ ,
nnoremap , <Nop>
xnoremap , <Nop>
onoremap , <Nop>

" -- C-x chord layer (workspace verbs: buffer, tab, file). Native decrement
" moves to <C-S-A>. <C-x> itself becomes a <Nop> prefix and waits for a second
" key, up to timeoutlen 5000.
"
" The whole C-x layer moved here from the IDE keymap ("Emacs Custom") in
" 2026-07, leaving zero C-x shortcuts on the IDE side. One C-x chord anywhere
" in the IDE keymap is enough to put the first keystroke into the IDE's
" chord-pending state, and the shell in the Terminal tool window then never
" receives C-x at all. Two concrete breakages that caused: emacs in the
" terminal fired CloseAllEditors on C-x C-c, which destroys editor state, and
" zsh lost C-x C-e (edit-command-line). With the layer in IdeaVim, C-x applies
" only inside the editor and the terminal keeps the whole key.
" Accepted cost: the C-x layer does not work inside tool windows. Esc or
" Ctrl+Esc returns to the editor in one step. There are no insert-mode chords,
" matching nvim, where the <C-x> mappings are normal-mode only.
" Note: IntelliJ IDEA's "Emacs Custom.xml" is the canonical copy. Every other
" JetBrains product carries a byte-for-byte copy of it, distributed by `cp`
" after editing IDEA's since 2026-07, so clearing C-x holds product-wide.
" Edit only IDEA's keymap, then copy.
nnoremap <C-S-A> <C-x>
noremap <C-x> <Nop>
" close family, mirroring lua/plugins/ui/bufferline.lua. k is the emacs
" kill-buffer spelling, O is Others/Only. The digit keys are all retired:
" 0/C-0/1/2/3 addressed panes, which now belong to <C-w>, and Ctrl-digit also
" depends on the CSI-u protocol in a terminal.
nmap <C-x>k <Action>(CloseContent)
nmap <C-x>O <Action>(CloseAllEditorsButActive)
nmap <C-x>[ <Action>(CloseAllToTheLeft)
nmap <C-x>] <Action>(CloseAllToTheRight)
" buffer and file navigation. n/p mirror nvim's BufferLineCycle. o mirrors
" nvim's <C-x>o pick-buffer, since Switcher is the closest JetBrains has to
" pick-and-jump. b is an emacs mnemonic alias for the same action
" (switch-to-buffer).
nmap <C-x>n <Action>(NextTab)
nmap <C-x>p <Action>(PreviousTab)
nmap <C-x>o <Action>(Switcher)
nmap <C-x>b <Action>(Switcher)
nmap <C-x><C-f> <Action>(GotoFile)
" Tab itself keeps the IDE's smart indent (EmacsStyleIndent, bound in the IDE
" keymap). Inserting a literal tab needs no invented chord, because vim already
" has quoted-insert i_CTRL-V / i_CTRL-Q (vim-engine's
" InsertCompletedLiteralAction; the conflict arbitration already awarded <C-q>
" to vim). In insert mode press <C-q><Tab>, or <C-q>009 if the IDE still grabs
" the raw Tab, since the numeric key code goes through the typed-char channel
" and is the most reliable form. Emacs spells quoted-insert C-q as well, so one
" set of muscle memory covers both, and nvim's native <C-v><Tab> means the two
" editors line up without configuration.
" Archived dead end, do not retry: imap <C-x><Tab> <Action>(EditorTab). The
" IDE action system consumes the chord's second keystroke as EmacsStyleIndent
" before IdeaVim sees it, so the pending mapping never completes. Any
" insert-mode chord ending in a key the IDE contests heavily (Tab, Enter, Esc)
" fails the same way.
" nvim-only, not mirrored: <C-x>K force close (the IDE autosaves, so it has no
" dirty concept), <C-x>d pick-and-close (no picker), the <C-x>t/T/<Tab>/X/R
" tabpage layer (JetBrains has no tabpages), <C-x>,/. move buffer, <C-x>`
" terminal, <C-x>m minimap.
" Stock emacs chords dropped during the migration because vim already covers
" them: C-x h (ggVG or Cmd-A), C-x x (visual o), C-x ` (]d, F2 kept), C-x C-s
" (:w, and autosave), C-x Tab indent (the > operator), C-x C-c (dangerous, see
" above), C-x C-o (rare, use the menu).


" -- Tab management asymmetry
" JetBrains has no equivalent of vim's tabpage as a workspace container. An IDE
" tab is file-level, roughly a vim buffer, and every file tab in a project
" window shares one tool-window layout. There is no way to keep a separate
" split layout and name per tab the way vim does. These nvim keys are therefore
" left unbound here on purpose:
"   <C-x>R    rename the current tab (lua/core/keymaps.lua stores t:tabname and
"             bufferline renders it through custom_areas). JetBrains has no
"             nameable workspace unit. The closest thing is saving a tool
"             window layout ("Window > Store/Restore Layout"), which means
"             something different; mapping it anyway would only mislead.
" The tab create/switch keys (<C-x>T, <C-x><Tab>, <C-x><S-Tab>, <C-x>X) are
" unbound for the same reason. On JetBrains, "New Window" (a separate IDE
" window) plus file tabs already cover most of what they were for.

" -- Terminal asymmetry (nvim-only)
" JetBrains' Terminal tool window is a separate non-modal surface that never
" goes through IdeaVim. The IDE handles its keys directly, so vim keys and
" shell keys never compete for the same keystroke. None of the nvim design
" (lua/plugins/ui/toggleterm.lua and ui/flatten.lua) is mirrored:
"   <C-x>`    toggleterm on/off. The IDE uses Opt-F12 or the Terminal tool window.
"   <C-]>     escape hatch out of Terminal mode. nvim needs it only because its
"             terminal is modal. Every other key reaches the shell untouched,
"             so Esc, Meta, fzf and lazygit are never intercepted.
"   flatten   forwards `nvim file` and git commit from inside the terminal to
"             the host so sessions do not nest. In the IDE terminal those
"             already open in the editor area, so the problem does not exist.

" -- Window / split navigation (mirrors vim-tmux-navigator: lua/plugins/ui/tmux.lua).
" IdeaVim implements vim's <C-w>{h,j,k,l} window commands natively. Verified
" against vim-engine's own generated command registry rather than the docs:
" WindowLeftAction, WindowDownAction, WindowUpAction, WindowRightAction, mode
" N. Remapping Ctrl+hjkl onto them covers the "move focus across editor splits"
" half of what vim-tmux-navigator does on the nvim side. Confirmed in-editor.
" <C-h>/<C-l> also chain Diff.FocusOppositePane. Diff panes are embedded
" editors rather than windows, so <C-w>h/l cannot reach them. nvim needs no
" counterpart because diffview panes there are real windows.
" Measured 2026-08-21: inside a diff, bare <C-h>/<C-l> toggle panes and never
" leave the tab. Leave with <C-w>hjkl, whose second key takes no user mapping
" and so bypasses the chain.
" Toggle semantics: FocusOppositePane is the only pane action, direction is
" not honored. Reaches the component-local action via IdeaVim's EmptyAction
" fallback (IjActionExecutor.getAction); if pane switching breaks after an
" IdeaVim update, look there. nmap: <Action> needs recursive maps.
" <C-j>/<C-k> stay pure, pane layouts are horizontal only.
nmap <C-h> <C-w>h<Action>(Diff.FocusOppositePane)
nnoremap <C-j> <C-w>j
nnoremap <C-k> <C-w>k
nmap <C-l> <C-w>l<Action>(Diff.FocusOppositePane)
" No tool-window half: IdeaVim/IntelliJ has no directional "focus the tool window
" to the left/below" action, only fixed-target activators (Alt+1..9 /
" Activate<Name>ToolWindow, already covered by <leader>v* below) and
" non-directional focus toggles (Esc -> back to editor, F12 -> jump to last tool
" window). Unresolved upstream, confirmed via three separate open YouTrack issues:
" VIM-3302, VIM-3152, VIM-1042. Not faking a directional binding here. There is
" nothing correct to bind it to.

" -- Window lifecycle: vim-native <C-w> is the single grammar on both sides.
" vim-engine implements <C-w>{h,j,k,l,s,v,c,q,o,w} natively (verified against
" vim-engine action/window sources), so split/close/only/cycle need no mapping
" here or on the nvim side. <C-x> stays a workspace-layer prefix (tab/buffer);
" the emacs-style C-x 2/3 split chords are retired on BOTH sides. nvim's
" <C-x>2/3 are deleted (lua/core/keymaps.lua), and the IDE-keymap C-x split
" chords are stripped from "Emacs Custom" (SplitHorizontally / SplitVertically /
" UnsplitAll / NextSplitter inherited from stock Emacs, plus the custom
" C-x C-2, C-x C-3, C-x alt-o). Ctrl-digit second keystrokes were untypable in
" terminals anyway, which is what forced the two sides apart.
"
" Gap-filling maps for <C-w> subcommands vim-engine does NOT implement:
"   <C-w>L / <C-w>J  move split right/down, mirroring nvim's wincmd L/J, which
"     is bound there as <C-w><S-L>/<S-J> for the kitty keyboard protocol.
"     Semantics verified in platform SplitAction.java: MoveTabRight/Down always
"     split the current group and carry the tab into the new half (forceSplit).
"     They spawn splits, they do not move around a grid, and they are disabled
"     while the group holds a single tab (the tabCount >= 2 guard). That is
"     also why upstream has no MoveTabLeft/Up: a left or up spawn mirrors the
"     right/down layout, so only one canonical direction ships.
"     <C-w>H/K stay unbound. The one action that moves into an EXISTING group,
"     MoveEditorToOppositeTabGroup, ignores direction and handles two-group
"     layouts only, so a directional key would move the wrong way half the
"     time. It lives on <C-w>x instead.
"   <C-w>x  an approximation. vim exchanges two windows; the action only moves
"     the editor to the opposite group. Closest semantics available.
nmap <C-w>L <Action>(MoveTabRight)
nmap <C-w>J <Action>(MoveTabDown)
nmap <C-w>x <Action>(MoveEditorToOppositeTabGroup)
" Resize is fully native since 2.39, verified in frontend_commands.json:
" <C-w>+/- height, <C-w>>/< width, <C-w>= equalise, <C-w>_/| maximise, and
" :resize / :vertical resize [±n]. The submode below shadows four of those
" keys, > < + -, with equivalent semantics plus repeat. = _ | and the ex
" commands still reach the native implementation directly.
"
" Hydra-style repeat, mirroring nvim's lua/plugins/ui/hydra.lua. This is a
" pending-<Plug> submode, the vim-submode technique. Each step appends the
" pseudo-key <Plug>(wres), and `notimeout` leaves it pending for the next key.
" > < + - = match, so they adjust once more and go pending again. Any other key
" fails to match, the pseudo-key is discarded, and that key executes normally,
" which exits the submode. Bare keys inside the submode can be held down,
" because every keydown from OS auto-repeat hits the pending mapping.
" The entry mappings use ex commands rather than native <C-w>>, because nmap is
" recursive and an RHS containing its own LHS raises E223.
" Accepted cost: a count prefix like 5<C-w>> stops working, since the 5 gets
" eaten as the range of the `:` command.
" Entry, where the first keystroke already performs the first adjustment:
nmap <C-w>> :vertical resize +1<CR><Plug>(wres)
nmap <C-w><lt> :vertical resize -1<CR><Plug>(wres)
nmap <C-w>+ :resize +1<CR><Plug>(wres)
nmap <C-w>- :resize -1<CR><Plug>(wres)
" Inside the submode: bare keys, holdable. = equalises natively and stays in.
nmap <Plug>(wres)> :vertical resize +1<CR><Plug>(wres)
nmap <Plug>(wres)<lt> :vertical resize -1<CR><Plug>(wres)
nmap <Plug>(wres)+ :resize +1<CR><Plug>(wres)
nmap <Plug>(wres)- :resize -1<CR><Plug>(wres)
nmap <Plug>(wres)= <C-w>=<Plug>(wres)
" nvim-only, intentionally unbound here:
"   <C-w>T            break window out into a new tabpage. JetBrains has no
"                     tabpage concept (see Tab management asymmetry above);
"                     MoveTabToNewWindow detaches a separate IDE window,
"                     different semantics, not bound.

" -- LSP hover / signature help: both sides use community defaults
" normal K          → Quick Documentation  (IdeaVim native)
" insert <C-k>      → ParameterInfo         (IDE, not intercepted by IdeaVim;
"                                            bind in IDE Keymap if the default
"                                            Cmd/Ctrl+P has been overridden)
" Mirror of lua/core/lsp.lua. Intentionally unbound here, nothing to do.

" -- easymotion
" Mirrors ~/.config/nvim/lua/plugins/edit/motion.lua (Flash.nvim).
" <leader><leader>t/T on the Neovim side is Flash Treesitter, intentionally
" left unbound here since IdeaVim has no equivalent node-based jump.

" Line direction (words, current line)
map <leader><leader>h <Plug>(easymotion-linebackward)
map <leader><leader>l <Plug>(easymotion-lineforward)

" Buffer-wide word (mirror of h/l, word = \k not \S+)
map <leader><leader>H <Plug>(easymotion-b)
map <leader><leader>L <Plug>(easymotion-w)

" Cross-line first-non-blank
map <leader><leader>j <Plug>(easymotion-j)
map <leader><leader>k <Plug>(easymotion-k)

" Line-scoped word motions
map <leader><leader>w <Plug>(easymotion-wl)
map <leader><leader>b <Plug>(easymotion-bl)
map <leader><leader>e <Plug>(easymotion-el)

" Buffer-wide WORD motions (non-whitespace blocks)
map <leader><leader>W <Plug>(easymotion-W)
map <leader><leader>B <Plug>(easymotion-B)
map <leader><leader>E <Plug>(easymotion-E)

" Incremental label jump (2-char / label search)
map <leader><leader>s <Plug>(easymotion-s2)
map <leader><leader>/ <Action>(AceAction)

" Repeat last jump
map <leader><leader>. <Plug>(easymotion-repeat)

nmap <Plug>(easymotion-prefix) <Nop>

map <A-n> <Action>(SelectNextOccurrence)
map <A-S-n> <Action>(SelectAllOccurrences)
" <A-x> is matchSkipCursor on the multicursor.nvim side: skip the current match
" and select the next one. IdeaVim's multiple-cursors emulation ships
" <Plug>SkipOccurrence for this (see the multiple-cursors section of the
" official emulated-plugins doc), so this goes through <Plug>, not <Action>.
map <A-x> <Plug>SkipOccurrence
map <A-p> <Action>(UnselectPreviousOccurrence)
" nvim-side asymmetry: <A-p> is also bound as a buffer-local "toggle preview popup"
" inside the snacks.explorer list (lua/plugins/ui/snacks.lua) and the trouble list
" (lua/plugins/ui/trouble.lua). Both are sidebar tools that don't exist as IdeaVim
" surfaces. JetBrains has its own Project tool window and Problems view, so there's
" no IDE Action to mirror here. Multi-cursor semantics are unaffected: those buffers
" don't accept multi-cursor anyway, so the buffer-local override is invisible outside
" them. Recorded here so the parity map stays a single source of truth for <A-p>.


map  <A-j>   <Action>(EditorCloneCaretBelow)
map  <A-k>   <Action>(EditorCloneCaretAbove)


nmap <leader>/ <Action>(FindInPath)
nmap <leader>, <Action>(RecentFiles)

" Structure / Outline. Mirrors lua/plugins/ui/aerial.lua on the Neovim side.
" <leader>ns is the transient popup (Navigation extras namespace, no g* equivalent).
" The persistent sidebar lives at <leader>vs in section "3) Views" below. Its
" semantics is "open a tool window", which is the Views-namespace contract.
nmap <leader>ns <Action>(FileStructurePopup)

" Search prefix <leader>s* is nvim-only (lua/plugins/ui/snacks.lua +
" lua/plugins/edit/rip-substitute.lua + lua/plugins/edit/grug-far.lua):
"   <leader>ss  workspace symbols     Shift+Shift (Search Everywhere)
"   <leader>sc  commands / actions    Shift+Shift, "Actions" tab
"   <leader>sk  keymaps picker        Help > Find Action
"   <leader>sb  buffer lines fuzzy    Ctrl+F (Find in File) + fuzzy mode
"   <leader>sB  grep open buffers     Edit > Find > Find in Files (scope: open)
"   <leader>sw  grep word/selection   Ctrl+Shift+F on cursor word
"   <leader>s/  search history        the Find toolbar dropdown keeps history
"   <leader>sr  rip-substitute        Ctrl+R (Replace, current file)
"   <leader>sR  grug-far              Ctrl+Shift+R (Replace in Path)
" IdeaVim deliberately doesn't mirror these: Search Everywhere + native Find/
" Replace already cover the same ground with a single entry point, so
" duplicating them as leader bindings would just add noise.
"
" Note: the nvim side splits replace into two scopes that JetBrains keeps as
" two shortcuts. <leader>sr (rip-substitute, current buffer, PCRE2 popup over
" vim's native :s) mirrors Ctrl+R, and <leader>sR (grug-far, workspace-wide,
" editable result buffer with file-filter/paths/flags) mirrors Ctrl+Shift+R.
" IDE side has no PCRE2-vs-vim :s split. Ctrl+R supports regex out of the box.


" Mapping IDE actions: https://jb.gg/abva3t

" 1)  Refactoring
" Anything that must also work on a selection uses `map`, not `nmap`.
" <leader>ca = quick-fixes + intentions + code actions (mirror of
" vim.lsp.buf.code_action in lua/core/lsp.lua). Kept outside <leader>r*
" because that namespace is reserved for deterministic refactorings.
" nvim additionally binds <A-CR> to the same code_action, mirroring the
" IDE's native Alt-Enter. That shortcut already works here without
" any IdeaVim mapping, so nothing to bind on this side.
map <leader>ca <Action>(ShowIntentionActions)
map <leader>R <Action>(RefactoringMenu)
map <leader>rn <Action>(RenameElement)
nmap <leader>rs <Action>(ChangeSignature)
map <leader>rv <Action>(IntroduceVariable)
map <leader>rc <Action>(IntroduceConstant)
map <leader>rf <Action>(IntroduceField)
map <leader>rm <Action>(ExtractMethod)
map <leader>ri <Action>(ExtractInterface)
map <leader>rl <Action>(Inline)
map <leader>rd <Action>(SafeDelete)
nmap <leader>rp <Action>(IntroduceParameter)
nmap <leader>rj <Action>(ConvertPythonToJupyterAction)
nmap <leader>rr <Action>(RefactorCode)
map <leader>rM <Action>(Move)


" 2) Navigation. Aligned with Neovim/LSP community g* defaults.
" Overriding Vim natives here:
"   gi  "insert at last insert position" (use <C-o>i or `^i instead)
"   gr  virtual replace mode (use R for normal replace)
"   gD  search word in current file (use * / # instead)
" gd is already IdeaVim default (GotoDeclaration); declared here for symmetry.
nmap gd <Action>(GotoDeclaration)
nmap gD <Action>(GotoTypeDeclaration)
nmap gi <Action>(GotoImplementation)
nmap gr <Action>(FindUsages)


" <leader>n*, extras that have no standard g* default:
"   nb  → supertypes / GotoSuperClass
"   nt  → GotoTest
"   nf  → FindInPath (search, not jump)
"   ns  → structure popup (transient; persistent sidebar at <leader>vs)
" GotoSuperMethod intentionally unbound: LSP's typeHierarchy only covers
" class-level, not method-level. Keep parity with the Neovim side.
"
" Asymmetry, Neovim only:
"   <leader>n{h,j,k,l} → treewalker.nvim AST walker (sticky hydra)
"     h=parent  j=next sibling  k=prev sibling  l=drill into child
"   No JetBrains equivalent. The IDE has Move Statement Up/Down (Cmd+Shift+Up/Down)
"   for swap but no AST sibling/parent walk. Not bound here; reserve
"   <leader>n{h,j,k,l} on the IDE side for possible future use.
map <leader>nb <Action>(GotoSuperClass)
map <leader>nt <Action>(GotoTest)
map <leader>nf <Action>(FindInPath)

"3) Views

nmap <leader>vp <Action>(ActivateProjectToolWindow)
" Asymmetry: snacks.explorer on Neovim binds vim-fold-style z[oOcCaARM] inside
" the tree (lua/plugins/ui/snacks.lua). The JetBrains Project tool window uses
" the IDE's own keymap (Right/Left, NumPad +/-, Ctrl+NumPad+ for expand-all),
" which IdeaVim cannot intercept. Leave it to the IDE keymap.
nmap <leader>vs <Action>(ActivateStructureToolWindow)
nmap <leader>vc <Action>(ActivateCommitToolWindow)
nmap <leader>vr <Action>(ActivateRunToolWindow)
nmap <leader>vd <Action>(ActivateDebugToolWindow)
nmap <leader>vt <Action>(ActivateTODOToolWindow)
nmap <leader>vP <Action>(ActivateProblemsViewToolWindow)
" vD and vH mirror diffview (lua/plugins/git/diffview.lua). Accepted difference
" in scope: nvim's vD diffs the whole repo tree (DiffviewOpen <base>...HEAD),
" while IDEA's CompareWithBranch compares the current file against the chosen
" ref. For a whole-repo comparison use the Git.Branches popup and Compare.
" Both sides still mean "pick a base, look at the diff".
nmap <leader>vD <Action>(Git.CompareWithBranch)
nmap <leader>vH <Action>(Vcs.ShowTabbedFileHistory)
nmap <leader>vz <Action>(ToggleDistractionFreeMode)
nmap <leader>vF <Action>(ToggleFullScreen)
nmap <leader>vm <Action>(ActivateBookmarksToolWindow)
" Views panel toggles that mirror Neovim:
"   vp → Snacks.explorer            (lua/plugins/ui/snacks.lua)
"   vs → :AerialToggle!             (lua/plugins/ui/aerial.lua)
"   vr → :OverseerToggle            (lua/plugins/runtime/overseer.lua)
"   vd → require'dapui'.toggle      (lua/plugins/runtime/dap.lua)
"   vt → :TodoTrouble                (lua/plugins/ui/todo-comments.lua)
"        nvim also exposes a Snacks picker at <leader>st. The mental model is
"        that Search uses a picker and View uses a panel. IdeaVim has no
"        <leader>s* namespace, since Search Everywhere replaces it, so only
"        vt = panel is mirrored.
"   vP → :Trouble diagnostics ...     (lua/plugins/ui/trouble.lua)
"   vm → :BookmarksTree (alias of <leader>M) for cross-editor parity.
"        Canonical entry on the nvim side is <leader>M (master-singleton
"        convention: uppercase single letter = total/master menu, mirrors
"        <leader>R for refactoring). <leader>vm is an alias so JetBrains
"        muscle memory still works on the nvim side.
"        Picker form lives at <leader>sm. That whole <leader>s* namespace is
"        nvim-only, since the IDE uses Search Everywhere instead. It runs on
"        the snacks picker; the old telescope hard-dep worry is retired.
" Asymmetries (intentional, not bound on the nvim side):
"   vc  → Commit Tool Window  (nvim uses lazygit via <localleader>G instead)
"   vz  → Distraction Free    (no zen-mode plugin installed)
"   vF  → FullScreen          (terminal-multiplexer level, out of nvim scope)
" nvim-only extras with no IdeaVim equivalent:
"   vn  → Snacks.notifier.show_history  (lua/plugins/ui/snacks.lua)
"   vM  → toggle code minimap           (lua/plugins/ui/neominimap.lua)
"        On JetBrains the equivalent is the CodeGlance plugin (or built-in
"        Settings > Editor > General > Appearance > "Show code lens on the
"        scrollbar"); both render in the gutter outside IdeaVim's reach, so
"        no <leader>vM mirror here. Default-on on the nvim side to match
"        CodeGlance's always-visible behavior.
"   <C-x>m → toggle focus into/out of minimap (nvim-only; <C-x> chord prefix
"        is IDE-routed, see top-of-file note. JetBrains has no native
"        equivalent, since the minimap there is not a focusable window.)


" 4)  Generate
" Two tiers:
"   <leader>G + the static maps below = language-AGNOSTIC actions. These are
"     platform CodeInsightActions (one ID, the IDE routes to the current file's
"     language handler). No per-language wiring needed. Confirmed via gh in
"     JetBrains/intellij-community: OverrideMethods/ImplementMethods register in
"     platform/.../idea/LangActions.xml, not in any language-specific plugin.
"   <leader>g{c,G,S,A,m,J,j} = CONCEPT keys: the same key generates the right thing per
"     language. Dispatched by filetype (see the augroup) because (a) IdeaVim's
"     vimscript can't read &filetype/% and (b) <buffer>-local maps are unsupported
"     Instead we re-assert GLOBAL maps on BufEnter. Needs IdeaVim >= 2.33.0 (autocmd,
"     2026-04 / VIM-1693).
"
" Cross-editor asymmetry (IdeaVim-only on purpose): Neovim has no <leader>g* MENU,
" but it does generate code, just through other surfaces, chiefly <leader>ca =
" vim.lsp.buf.code_action (lua/core/lsp.lua), plus friendly-snippets (blink) and
" refactoring.nvim's extract (<leader>r*). Coverage is LSP-dependent: rust-analyzer
" is rich (getter/setter/new/derive all as code actions), gopls is leaner (Fill-
" struct / Add-tags / Stub-methods, but NO constructor/getter/setter). So JetBrains'
" single structured Generate menu (Alt+Insert = <leader>G/<leader>g*) has no 1:1
" Neovim mirror: Neovim folds generation into the code-action surface (Alt+Enter
" intentions = <leader>ca) and what's offered varies by language server. That's why
" the generate tier (<leader>G/go/gi/gd + concept keys below) lives only here.
" Nothing is missing on the nvim side; the two use different surface models.
" The two surfaces overlap more than that split suggests: GoLand also serves
" getter/setter as an Alt+Enter intention, which is the surface that fires on a
" single field. So <leader>ca reaches the same generator on BOTH sides, and it is
" the escape hatch whenever a concept key goes silent (see fact 3 below).
"
" gt/gT/gu have Neovim counterparts, all wired in
" lua/plugins/edit/wrap.lua (engine/templates in lua/tools/wrap.lua):
"   gt/gT → template picker over the selection. One menu serves both keys: the
"     SurroundWith vs SurroundWithLiveTemplate split collapses. Templates live
"     in one table. Works in visual AND normal mode (normal = wrap current
"     line, same as the IDE with no selection).
"   gu → tools/wrap.lua M.unwrap(): caret anywhere inside the block, same as
"     the IDE (treesitter walks up to the enclosing construct; the innermost
"     one at the caret is unwrapped, so depth replaces the IDE's level popup).
"     Brace/keyword languages delegate to deleft.vim; python unwraps in Lua
"     (deleft's python path is synID-based and silently no-ops under
"     treesitter highlighting). Multi-branch: python keeps the branch under
"     the caret + finally, drops the rest (= Unwrap if / Unwrap else / drop
"     handlers); matchit languages keep all bodies (deleft strategy=none).
map <leader>G  <Action>(Generate)
map <leader>go <Action>(OverrideMethods)
map <leader>gi <Action>(ImplementMethods)
map <leader>gd <Action>(GenerateDelegate)
map <leader>gt <Action>(SurroundWith)
map <leader>gT <Action>(SurroundWithLiveTemplate)
map <leader>gu <Action>(Unwrap)

" Concept-key dispatch. Per-filetype generators for the same keys:
"   gc constructor · gG getter · gS setter · gA getter+setter · gm method
"   gJ/gj type/struct-from-JSON. Go-only concepts, no cross-language meaning.
" Only Go + JS/TS expose standalone generator action IDs; everywhere else the
" concept key falls back to the generic Generate popup (set in the `BufEnter *`
" reset). IDs re-verified 2026-08-21: Go against the plugin's own
" GoBundle.properties (`action.GoGenerate*Action.text`), JS against
" intellij-community's actionsUsagesV3.csv. Harvest current IDs with
" `:actionlist <pattern>`. The Go plugin is closed source, so the running IDE
" is the only authority.
"
" The `BufEnter *` reset is load-bearing, not decoration: `set commentary` owns
" `gc`, so an UNMAPPED <leader>gc would fall through to `l` plus a pending
" comment operator that swallows the next keystroke. Never delete a concept key
" without deleting its reset line too.
"
" Sourcing facts. These were open questions when the tier was written, and all
" three are now settled against IdeaVim 2.46 sources, don't re-derive them:
"   1) A pattern is ONE glob, never a comma list. AutoCmdPattern.toRegex emits a
"      literal `,` outside `{}`, so `*.js,*.ts` compiles to a regex demanding a
"      comma in the filename and matches nothing. The JS/TS branch below was
"      dead from the day it was written. Brace alternation is the documented
"      multi-extension form.
"   2) BufEnter fires on every buffer switch, including returning to an
"      already-open tab, so a concept key cannot stay on the previous file's
"      generator. FileType is not needed.
"   3) A disabled or renamed action is SILENT. `<Action>()` routes to
"      IjActionExecutor.executeAction(editor, name, ctx), which returns false
"      without a message; only the `:action` Ex command raises "Action not
"      found". So a key that does nothing means either the caret sits somewhere
"      the generator rejects, or the ID died. `:action <id>` tells them apart.

" Seed the concept keys statically. The augroup only specializes them once
" BufEnter has fired, so a freshly sourced rc leaves them UNMAPPED until the next
" tab switch. That reads as "the key does nothing" and, for <leader>gc, drops
" through to commentary's operator. Seeding costs 7 lines and removes that window.
" nmap (not map) so the augroup's nmap always overrides the same mode.
nmap <leader>gc <Action>(Generate)
nmap <leader>gG <Action>(Generate)
nmap <leader>gS <Action>(Generate)
nmap <leader>gA <Action>(Generate)
nmap <leader>gm <Action>(Generate)
nmap <leader>gJ <Action>(Generate)
nmap <leader>gj <Action>(Generate)

" `autocmd!` first → re-sourcing the rc replaces handlers instead of stacking them.
augroup GenerateConceptDispatch
  autocmd!
  " every buffer → reset concept keys to the generic, language-aware Generate popup
  autocmd BufEnter * nmap <leader>gc <Action>(Generate)
  autocmd BufEnter * nmap <leader>gG <Action>(Generate)
  autocmd BufEnter * nmap <leader>gS <Action>(Generate)
  autocmd BufEnter * nmap <leader>gA <Action>(Generate)
  autocmd BufEnter * nmap <leader>gm <Action>(Generate)
  autocmd BufEnter * nmap <leader>gJ <Action>(Generate)
  autocmd BufEnter * nmap <leader>gj <Action>(Generate)
  " Go (GoLand). Full set including the Go-only JSON generators.
  " gG/gS/gA act on ONE field: the caret must be on a struct field name, not on
  " the type name (GoBundle: "...getter and setter methods for a field").
  autocmd BufEnter *.go nmap <leader>gc <Action>(GoGenerateConstructorAction)
  autocmd BufEnter *.go nmap <leader>gG <Action>(GoGenerateGetterAction)
  autocmd BufEnter *.go nmap <leader>gS <Action>(GoGenerateSetterAction)
  autocmd BufEnter *.go nmap <leader>gA <Action>(GoGenerateGetterSetterAction)
  autocmd BufEnter *.go nmap <leader>gm <Action>(GoGenerateMethodAction)
  autocmd BufEnter *.go nmap <leader>gJ <Action>(GoGenerateTypeFromJsonAction)
  autocmd BufEnter *.go nmap <leader>gj <Action>(GoGenerateStructFieldsFromJsonAction)
  " JS / TS. Standalone Generate.*.JavaScript IDs, which cover .ts too. Brace
  " alternation, not a comma list. See fact 1 above.
  autocmd BufEnter *.{js,jsx,mjs,cjs,ts,tsx,mts,cts} nmap <leader>gc <Action>(Generate.Constructor.JavaScript)
  autocmd BufEnter *.{js,jsx,mjs,cjs,ts,tsx,mts,cts} nmap <leader>gG <Action>(Generate.GetAccessor.JavaScript)
  autocmd BufEnter *.{js,jsx,mjs,cjs,ts,tsx,mts,cts} nmap <leader>gS <Action>(Generate.SetAccessor.JavaScript)
  autocmd BufEnter *.{js,jsx,mjs,cjs,ts,tsx,mts,cts} nmap <leader>gA <Action>(Generate.GetSetAccessor.JavaScript)
augroup END

" which-key labels. `set which-key` (top of file) falls back to showing the raw
" <Action>(...) rhs when a key has no g:WhichKeyDesc_* variable. That is what
" made this tier unreadable in the popup. Format is "<key sequence> <label>";
" the variable suffix only needs to be unique. Labels are user-facing, so
" English, the same rule as vim.notify text on the Neovim side.
let g:WhichKeyDesc_generate             = "<leader>g Generate / surround"
let g:WhichKeyDesc_generate_menu        = "<leader>G Generate... (Alt+Insert)"
let g:WhichKeyDesc_generate_override    = "<leader>go Override methods"
let g:WhichKeyDesc_generate_implement   = "<leader>gi Implement methods"
let g:WhichKeyDesc_generate_delegate    = "<leader>gd Delegate methods"
let g:WhichKeyDesc_generate_constructor = "<leader>gc Constructor"
let g:WhichKeyDesc_generate_getter      = "<leader>gG Getter (caret on a field)"
let g:WhichKeyDesc_generate_setter      = "<leader>gS Setter (caret on a field)"
let g:WhichKeyDesc_generate_getset      = "<leader>gA Getter + setter (caret on a field)"
let g:WhichKeyDesc_generate_method      = "<leader>gm Method"
let g:WhichKeyDesc_generate_type_json   = "<leader>gJ Type from JSON (Go)"
let g:WhichKeyDesc_generate_fields_json = "<leader>gj Struct fields from JSON (Go)"
let g:WhichKeyDesc_generate_surround    = "<leader>gt Surround with..."
let g:WhichKeyDesc_generate_surround_t  = "<leader>gT Surround with template..."
let g:WhichKeyDesc_generate_unwrap      = "<leader>gu Unwrap"

" 5)  Git. <localleader>g*, mirroring gitsigns and git-conflict
"     (lua/plugins/git/). Every action ID here was read out of the open-source
"     VcsActions.xml and intellij.vcs.git.backend.xml. Since the move to
"     all-in-IDEA the source is directly readable, so the old "closed-source
"     GoLand, cannot verify" caveat no longer applies.
"
" ]c/[c already live in the Bracket-prefix section. In a normal editor they pop
" the gutter toolbar, matching nvim's gs.nav_hunk preview; in a diff viewer
" they jump between chunks. No extra key needed.
nmap ,gp <Action>(VcsShowCurrentChangeMarker)
" ,gb differs in meaning, and that is accepted. nvim's blame_line pops a single
" line; Annotate toggles a gutter column, and pressing again closes it. The IDE
" has no single-line blame popup action.
nmap ,gb <Action>(Annotate)
nmap ,gr <Action>(Vcs.RollbackChangedLines)
nmap ,gd <Action>(Compare.SameVersion)
" ,gx: nvim puts conflicts in the quickfix list. The IDE opens the Resolve
" Conflicts dialog listing conflicted files, and double-clicking one enters the
" merge tool. Resolving hunk by hunk (nvim's co/ct/cb/c0 and ]x/[x, all done
" inside the buffer) belongs to the merge dialog on the IDE side, so nothing
" mirrors it in the editor.
nmap ,gx <Action>(Git.ResolveConflicts)
nmap ,G <Action>(Vcs.QuickListPopupAction)
" nvim-only, deliberately unbound because no equivalent action exists:
"   ,gs  stage/unstage hunk toggle. The IDE has no hunk-level stage action ID;
"        Git.Stage.Add works on whole files and does not toggle. Use the stage
"        button in the gutter toolbar, or the Commit tool window (<leader>vc).
"   ,gB  switch the gutter diff base. IDEA's gutter always compares against
"        HEAD or the index and the base cannot be changed. For a one-off
"        comparison against an arbitrary ref use <leader>vD in the Views
"        section.
"   ,do  mini-diff overlay. The IDE's gutter popup already covers previewing.
" File history lives at <leader>vH in the Views section, mirroring diffview.

" 6)  Debug. Mirrors lua/plugins/runtime/dap.lua (nvim-dap)
"
" Shared bindings (both sides):
nmap <leader>D <Action>(DebugMainMenu)
nmap <leader>db <Action>(ToggleLineBreakpoint)
map <leader>dB <Action>(BreakpointActionsGroup)
"
" Asymmetry: step, inspect and frame controls are intentionally NOT bound here.
"
" Neovim binds these under <localleader> (= `,`) and attaches them ONLY for
" the duration of a DAP session (attach/detach via dap.listeners.on_session;
" see actions table in lua/plugins/runtime/dap.lua). That gives
" <localleader>{c,n,s,f,r,e,h,j,k,R,q,Q} their CLI-debugger meaning
" (continue / next / step / finish / repl / eval / hover / frame ↓↑ /
" restart / quit / detach) only while debugging.
"
" IdeaVim has no debugger lifecycle hook. No Action or Plug fires on session
" start/end, so the same "attach-while-debugging" trick is not reproducible.
" Static bindings to IDE Debug actions (Resume / StepOver / ...) would work but
" would permanently consume <localleader>{c,n,...} for an editor where the
" canonical debug surface is F-keys + Debug Tool Window. Leaving those keys
" free on the IDE side is the deliberate trade-off.
"
" nvim additionally auto-enters a sticky "step hydra" whenever the debugger
" stops (tools/debug_hydra.lua): bare n/s/f/c/u step, J/K frames, `.` back
" to exec point. Single-key repeats with an on-screen hint, <Esc> leaves,
" `,d` re-enters. nvim-only by design: JetBrains' step surface is already
" single-key (F7/F8/...), so there is nothing to mirror here.
"
" On JetBrains: F8 (StepOver) / F7 (StepInto) / Shift-F8 (StepOut) /
" F9 (Resume) / Alt-F8 (EvaluateExpression) / Alt-F9 (RunToCursor), plus the
" Debug Tool Window (<leader>vd above). Static <leader>dl (run last) and
" <leader>dq (terminate) on the nvim side have no mirror here. They are
" session-lifecycle conveniences that map cleanly to the IDE's own dropdown.

" 6b) Test / Run. nvim-only namespace; on JetBrains the Run/Debug Tool
" Window + run configurations cover the same workflow.
"   <leader>tt   neotest run nearest        (IDE: gutter run icon / Ctrl-Shift-F10)
"   <leader>tT   neotest run file
"   <leader>tl   neotest run last
"   <leader>tA   neotest run nearest with args (picker: ft preset + custom)
"   <leader>td   neotest debug nearest      (IDE: gutter debug icon)
"   <leader>ts   neotest summary toggle
"   <leader>to   neotest output for nearest
"   <leader>tO   neotest output panel
"   <leader>tS   neotest stop / drop stuck
"   <leader>tw   neotest watch toggle
"   <leader>tn   neotest jump to next failed
"   <leader>tp   neotest jump to prev failed
"   <leader>or   overseer run task          (IDE: Run Anything / run config dropdown)
"   <leader>oR   overseer rerun last
"   <leader>oo   overseer task action menu
"   <leader>oc   overseer cancel running

" 6c) Typst. nvim-only namespace (lua/plugins/lang/typst.lua + lsp/tinymist.lua).
" Typst itself doesn't ship as a JetBrains-blessed language; preview/format/LSP
" all run through tinymist on the Neovim side. On JetBrains use the third-party
" "Typst" plugin if installed. Its actions have no stable IDs to map here.
"
"   <localleader>tp   :TypstPreview                   start preview (browser, live)
"   <localleader>tt   :TypstPreviewToggle             toggle preview server
"   <localleader>tP   :TypstPreviewStop               stop preview server
"   <localleader>ts   :TypstPreviewSyncCursor         scroll preview to cursor (one-shot)
"   <localleader>tF   :TypstPreviewFollowCursorToggle toggle live cursor follow
"
" Format (.typ) goes through conform → typstyle on save (tools/mason_ensure.lua),
" same gateway as every other filetype, so <leader>ff still works.

" 6d) Scheme / Racket / Steel. Entirely nvim-only.
"
" JetBrains has no first-party Racket/Scheme/Steel environment with stable
" Action IDs to mirror. No REPL integration, no paredit equivalent, no
" structural-form motions. Rather than maintain a list of nvim keys that
" perpetually drifts (Conjure's defaults change, paredit's API evolves), we
" simply don't document them on the IDE side. The whole namespace is absent
" here intentionally.
"
" If you need to read Lisp source on JetBrains: install the third-party
" Racket plugin for syntax + indent + Reformat. Editing / REPL / LSP all
" belong on the nvim side. See lua/plugins/lang/scheme.lua and
" lsp/{racket_langserver,guile_lsp_server,steel_language_server}.lua.

" 6e) Markdown. <localleader>m* nvim-only namespace
" (lua/plugins/lang/markdown.lua; rendering split explained in
" lua/plugins/ui/snacks.lua image comments).
" JetBrains' bundled Markdown plugin already ships editor+preview split
" (layout buttons top-right of the editor; mermaid/math toggle in
" Settings > Languages > Markdown). It is the platform-level
" TextEditorWithPreview mechanism with no stable single Action ID to
" mirror, so no mapping here, same precedent as 6c) Typst.
"
"   <localleader>mr   render toggle, text + inline images follow (buffer)
"   <localleader>mR   render toggle, text + inline images follow (global)
"   (<localleader>ii  image-only on/off, <localleader>it inline↔float,
"    <localleader>ia{i,f,r}  allow blocked remote image/file/repo (trust
"    tiers; :ImageTrust to audit/clear). snacks.image, nvim-only; see
"    lua/tools/image_render.lua)
"   <localleader>mp   render-markdown preview (split)
"   <localleader>mb   :LivePreview start/close (toggle). Browser live
"                     preview, the only rendering outlet for mermaid / KaTeX
"                     math (deliberately NOT rendered in-terminal; no
"                     mmdc / TeX toolchain)

" 6f) AI / Claude Code. <leader>a* nvim-only namespace
" (lua/plugins/ai/claudecode.lua). nvim runs coder/claudecode.nvim, which
" speaks the SAME reverse-engineered WebSocket IDE protocol as the official
" Claude Code editor extensions, in `none` mode: nvim hosts the WS server,
" `claude` lives in a sibling tmux pane and attaches via its `/ide` picker.
" JetBrains uses the official Claude Code plugin's own tool window +
" keybindings. Same underlying protocol, but a tool-window plugin with no
" stable editor Action ID to mirror (same precedent as 6c Typst / 6e
" Markdown), so no <Action> mapping here.
"
" Keys: second letter = verb's first letter (Send/Buffer/Accept/Deny/Close/Info).
"   <leader>as   Send visual selection to Claude (visual mode)
"   <leader>ab   add Buffer (current file) to context
"   <leader>aa   Accept Claude's proposed diff
"   <leader>ad   Deny Claude's proposed diff
"   <leader>ac   Close all pending diffs
"   <leader>ai   connection Info (:ClaudeCodeStatus)

" 7) Reformat
map <leader>F <Action>(CodeFormatGroup)
map <leader>ff <Action>(ReformatCode)
map <leader>fF <Action>(ShowReformatFileDialog)
map <leader>fS <Action>(osmedile.intellij.stringmanip.PopupChoiceAction)

" 8)  Mark. Mirrors lua/plugins/edit/marks.lua
"
" Core bindings (mirrored on both sides):
nmap <leader>M <Action>(Bookmarks)
nmap <leader>mm <Action>(ToggleBookmark)
nmap <leader>mM <Action>(ToggleBookmarkWithMnemonic)
nmap <leader>mn <Action>(GotoNextBookmarkInEditor)
nmap <leader>mN <Action>(GotoPreviousBookmark)
"
" Search namespace, nvim-only (the <leader>s* namespace itself is nvim-only
" because IDE uses Search Everywhere); search by keyword, picker UI:
"   <leader>sm  → :BookmarksGoto      fuzzy picker over bookmarks
"                                     (snacks backend, set explicitly in
"                                      lua/plugins/edit/marks.lua)
"
" nvim-only extras (no precise IdeaVim equivalent; the <leader>M IDE panel
" covers similar ground interactively):
"   <leader>ml  → :BookmarksLists     switch active list (nvim multi-list)
"   <leader>ma  → :BookmarksCommands  bookmark command palette
"   <leader>mg  → :BookmarksGrep      grep inside bookmarked files

" AI
map <leader>A <Action>(AIIntentionsActionGroup)

" Popups and menus

nnoremap <leader>y "+y
vnoremap <leader>y "+y
nnoremap <leader>p "+p
vnoremap <leader>p "+p

nnoremap <A-d> "_d
vnoremap <A-d> "_d
nmap zj <Action>(NextFoldingSection)
nmap zk <Action>(PreviousFoldingSection)

" In visual mode, J and K move the selection.
vnoremap J :move '>+1<CR>gv=gv
vnoremap K :move '<-2<CR>gv=gv

nmap ]d <Action>(GotoNextError)
nmap [d <Action>(GotoPreviousError)

" ===== Syntax-aware navigation & editing =====
" Mirrors lua/plugins/edit/textobjects.lua (nvim-treesitter-textobjects on the
" Neovim side). IDE actions cover what JetBrains exposes natively; kinds
" without a matching action stay nvim-only (listed at the bottom of this
" section so both sides share one source of truth).

" Bracket-prefix motions
" Function jumps use native ]m/[m ]M/[M rather than <Action>(MethodDown/Up).
" The native commands are real motions, so d]f composes; they push the
" jumplist, so <C-o> comes back; and they read StructureView, which makes them
" language-agnostic and covers Python dialects too. Cost: every StructureView
" node counts as a landing spot, including classes and fields, so these are not
" strictly method-only.
map ]f ]m
map [f [m
map ]F ]M
map [F [M
" ]c/[c chain two contexts. In a normal editor the gutter marker pops the
" toolbar; in a diff viewer NextDiff/PreviousDiff jump between chunks, merge
" included. The two actions are mutually exclusive, because the marker action
" carries a platform !isDiffEditor guard, and the disabled one is skipped
" silently. They never both fire.
nmap ]c <Action>(VcsShowNextChangeMarker)<Action>(NextDiff)
nmap [c <Action>(VcsShowPrevChangeMarker)<Action>(PreviousDiff)

" Known gaps in the diff viewer (2026-08-21, not diagnosed and not being chased
" because the situation is rare): the PSI-backed keys ]f/[f, ]F/[F, af/if/aF
" stop working, and ]d/[d depend on inspections, which do not run in a diff.

" Only two text objects are remapped here, af/if/aF and iu. Every other key is
" left to the extensions' own init() defaults, on purpose: they all use
" IfMissing semantics and nothing contests those keys, so the result does not
" depend on load order. Hand-writing those maps in the rc would walk straight
" into point 1 of the ordering fact. These use omap and xmap rather than map,
" because a text object only means something in operator-pending and visual
" mode. The <Plug> names were read off the installed package, not copied from
" the docs.
"
" af/if come from functextobj (nvim binds af/if to @function.outer/inner).
" Intended side effect: its IfMissing test is hasMapTo(<Plug>), so it stops
" binding the am/aM/im defaults once these exist.
" Known mismatch, measured 2026-07 and accepted for now: functextobj's inner
" means "between the braces of the function body", which selects differently
" from nvim's @function.inner in Python. Use vin there to select a body. The
" built-in extensions iterate quickly, so retest after upgrades; if upstream
" starts taking the body per language, the two sides line up.
omap af <Plug>(textobj-function-am)
xmap af <Plug>(textobj-function-am)
omap if <Plug>(textobj-function-im)
xmap if <Plug>(textobj-function-im)
" aF selects the whole block including JavaDoc and annotations. nvim has no
" counterpart, so it sits on the uppercase key and costs no parity key.
omap aF <Plug>(textobj-function-aM)
xmap aF <Plug>(textobj-function-aM)
" iu is CamelCaseMotion's inner sub-word, keeping anyobject's old u key. There
" is no au, because the plugin offers inner only.
" The <Plug> names follow two different schemes, so do not mix them: the
" w/b/e/ge motions are <Plug>CamelCaseMotion_w, while text objects use the
" newer textObjects API and spell it <Plug>(CamelCaseMotion-iw).
omap iu <Plug>(CamelCaseMotion-iw)
xmap iu <Plug>(CamelCaseMotion-iw)

" Sibling swap (statement / argument).
" MoveStatementDown/Up is the same action IDE binds to Cmd+Shift+Down/Up.
" MoveElementRight/Left swaps a function argument with its neighbor.
nmap gss <Action>(MoveStatementDown)
nmap gsS <Action>(MoveStatementUp)
nmap gsa <Action>(MoveElementRight)
nmap gsA <Action>(MoveElementLeft)

" Incremental selection. Grow or shrink to the enclosing syntax node.
" <Action>(EditorSelectWord) through IdeaVim does NOT work: the dispatch
" resets the caret/selection model between calls, so the action's internal
" "extend further" state is lost and rapid presses all restart from cursor.
" Workaround: sethandler lets the IDE handle the key natively. Requires a
" one-time setup in IDE Preferences > Keymap:
"   Alt+W        → EditorSelectWord
"   Alt+Shift+W  → EditorUnSelectWord
" (IDE default is Opt+Up/Down on Mac; we rebind for parity with <A-w>/<A-W>.)
sethandler <A-w> a:ide
sethandler <A-W> a:ide

" Key ownership. This table is the single source of truth for the section.
"   af/if aF       functextobj(PSI)
"   ac             classtextobj (PSI, outer; inner is iC, lowercase ic unbound)
"   aC/iC ]C/[C    anyobject anyclass
"   ai/ii ]i/[i    anyobject anyconditional
"   al/il ]l/[l    anyobject anyloop
"   ak/ik ]k/[k    anyobject anycomment (no nvim counterpart, IdeaVim-only)
"   av/iv ]v/[v    anyobject anyvariable. av/iv have parity: nvim binds them to
"                  @assignment.outer and .rhs. Measured 2026-07 on
"                  `let rc: Rc<String> = Rc::new("hello".into());`, where both
"                  sides select the whole statement for av and
"                  `Rc::new("hello".into())` for iv.
"                  ]v/[v stay IdeaVim-only, because the treesitter side ships
"                  no assignment jump. nvim covers more file types instead:
"                  json, yaml and toml all have @assignment through
"                  treesitter, while AnyObject only recognises code.
"   ay/iy ]y/[y    anyobject anyitem (list items)
"   an/in          anyobject anyindentblock (no ]n/[n; only treesitter jumps)
"   aa/ia          argtextobj (arguments)
"   aq/iq ab/ib    mini-ai (quotes, brackets)
"   ae/ie          textobj-entire
"   iu             CamelCaseMotion inner sub-word
"   ]f/[f ]F/[F    native ]m/[m ]M/[M
"   [- ]- [+ ]+    indentwise, by indent level (no nvim counterpart, IdeaVim-only)
"   [= ]= [% ]%    same
"
" nvim-only, deliberately unbound because IdeaVim has no equivalent:
"   ]a/[a          @parameter.outer. argtextobj selects with aa/ia but offers
"                  no jump.
"   ]L/[L          @loop.outer end. anyobject's jumps only reach the start.
